{"input": {"topic": "data structures and algorithms", "role": "backend software engineer", "level": "mid-level", "mode": "explain", "question": "How should I approach a coding interview question about detecting a cycle in a linked list, and what follow-up questions might the interviewer ask?"}, "output": {"answer": "Start by clarifying the problem. Ask if the list is singly linked, if it can be empty, and whether you may modify nodes. Then state a brute force idea: store visited nodes in a hash set and return true if you see one twice. That is O(n) time and O(n) space. Next, offer the optimal approach, Floyd's tortoise and hare. Use a slow pointer that moves one step and a fast pointer that moves two steps. If they ever meet, there is a cycle. If fast reaches null, there is none. This is O(n) time and O(1) space. Walk through a small example, handle edge cases like an empty list or a single node, then code it and test it aloud. Interviewers often follow up by asking you to find the node where the cycle starts, compute the cycle length, or prove why the pointers must meet. To find the start, after the pointers meet, reset one pointer to the head and move both one step at a time. They meet at the cycle start.", "practice_questions": ["How would you find the node where the cycle begins, and why does the method work?", "How would you compute the length of the cycle once detected?", "What changes if the list is doubly linked or you may modify nodes?", "How would you detect a cycle in a stream of data or a graph?"], "tips": ["Name the brute force first, then improve it, so the interviewer sees your reasoning.", "State time and space complexity for each approach.", "Test with an empty list, one node, a self loop, and a long cycle.", "Practice explaining why the fast pointer cannot skip over the slow one inside a cycle."], "sources": ["Introduction to Algorithms (CLRS)", "Cracking the Coding Interview"], "uncertainty": ""}}
{"input": {"topic": "machine learning", "question": "teach me about machine learning"}, "output": {"answer": "Machine learning is about building models that learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules. For interviews, focus on the core ideas. Supervised learning uses labeled data for tasks like classification and regression. Unsupervised learning finds structure in unlabeled data, such as clustering. Reinforcement learning learns from rewards. A typical workflow is: define the problem, collect and clean data, split into train, validation, and test sets, choose a model, train it, evaluate with suitable metrics, and iterate. Key concepts to know are overfitting versus underfitting, the bias-variance tradeoff, regularization, cross-validation, and metrics like accuracy, precision, recall, and F1. Assuming a mid-level software engineer, aim to explain these simply and give a concrete example.", "practice_questions": ["What is the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning?", "How would you detect and reduce overfitting?", "When would you prefer precision over recall?", "How would you split data for training and evaluation?"], "tips": ["Explain each concept with a short real-world example.", "State the tradeoffs of any model you choose.", "Always mention how you would evaluate the model.", "Ask clarifying questions about data and goals first."], "sources": ["Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning by Christopher Bishop", "Hands-On Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn, Keras, and TensorFlow by Aurelien Geron", "scikit-learn official documentation"], "uncertainty": "The topic is broad, so I assumed a general overview. Tell me if you want a specific area, such as deep learning or ML system design."}}
{"input": {"topic": "SQL database", "question": "help with SQL database"}, "output": {"answer": "Happy to help with SQL for interviews. Your question is broad, so here is a general path. Start with core queries: SELECT, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, ORDER BY. Then learn joins (inner, left, right, full) and subqueries. Next, study indexes, transactions, and normalization. For each topic, practice explaining the idea, writing a query, and discussing trade-offs. Tell me your role, level, and mode (explain, practice, mock_interview, or feedback) and I will tailor this.", "practice_questions": ["What is the difference between INNER JOIN and LEFT JOIN? Give an example.", "Write a query to find the second highest salary in an Employees table.", "What is an index, and when can it hurt performance?", "Explain ACID properties and why transactions matter."], "tips": ["Clarify the table schema and edge cases such as NULLs and duplicates before writing a query.", "Talk through your reasoning out loud as you write SQL.", "Know the difference between WHERE and HAVING.", "Be ready to discuss query performance and indexing trade-offs."], "sources": ["Database System Concepts by Silberschatz, Korth, and Sudarshan", "Official documentation for PostgreSQL or MySQL"], "uncertainty": "The question is broad. I am not sure which SQL topic or mode you want, so I assumed a mid-level general software engineer."}}
{"input": {"topic": "machine learning vs artificial intelligence", "question": "What is the difference between machine learning and artificial intelligence?"}, "output": {"answer": "Artificial intelligence is the broad field of building systems that perform tasks we associate with human intelligence, such as reasoning, planning, perception, and language understanding. Machine learning is a subset of AI. It focuses on algorithms that learn patterns from data instead of following only hand-written rules. Deep learning is a further subset of machine learning that uses multi-layer neural networks. Not all AI uses machine learning. For example, rule-based expert systems and classic search algorithms are AI but not ML. In an interview, define AI first, place ML inside it, give one example of each, and mention a trade-off such as rules being predictable while learned models adapt to data but need data and monitoring.", "practice_questions": ["How would you explain supervised versus unsupervised learning to a non-technical person?", "Give an example of an AI system that does not use machine learning.", "When would you choose a rule-based approach over a machine learning model?"], "tips": ["Use a nested sets picture: AI contains ML, which contains deep learning.", "Always back definitions with a concrete example.", "Mention trade-offs such as data needs, explainability, and maintenance."], "sources": ["Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach by Russell and Norvig", "Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning by Bishop"], "uncertainty": ""}}

